  "prompt_text": "The figure illustrates how particles move through a flowing fluid and how interactions with the lower boundary affect that motion. In the main schematic, many red particles are carried generally from left to right, but their paths are not perfectly uniform. Near an obstacle inside the fluid, the arrows curve and scatter, highlighting a local “whirlpool effect” that disturbs the otherwise steady transport.\n\nTwo enlarged views emphasize boundary-related losses at the bottom surface. One shows a rough boundary, where the fluid-solid interface is jagged; particles traveling close to this uneven surface appear to be deflected or redirected by the irregular topography. The other zoom shows boundary leakage, where gaps or discontinuities in the boundary allow particles to pass downward out of the main flow region. Together, these close-ups suggest that both surface roughness and leakage degrade ideal transport near the edge.\n\nOverall, the image conveys a simple physical picture: while most particles follow the bulk flow, localized vortices, obstacles, and imperfect boundaries introduce scattering, trapping, or escape routes. The main comparison is between smooth, directed transport and disturbances caused by internal obstruction and nonideal boundary conditions.",
